# 🎯 Knowledge Transfer — EXHAUSTIVE Checklist
### Angular UI + Java Backend Project Takeover

> **Goal**: After completing every item below, you can independently develop, debug, deploy, operate, and maintain the entire project without the previous team.

---

## 📋 PHASE 1 — Project Context & Business Domain

### 1.1 Business Overview
- [ ] What does the system do? (elevator pitch)
- [ ] Who are the end users? (roles, personas, user segments)
- [ ] Who are the internal stakeholders? (PO, sponsor, department heads)
- [ ] What is the business model? (SaaS, internal tool, B2B, B2C)
- [ ] What is the current project phase? (MVP, growth, maintenance, sunset)
- [ ] What are the SLA / uptime requirements?
- [ ] What are the compliance requirements? (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI-DSS, SOC2)
- [ ] What regulatory constraints exist?
- [ ] How does this system fit in the org's broader ecosystem?
- [ ] What other systems depend on this system?
- [ ] What is the user base size? (concurrent users, peak traffic patterns)
- [ ] What are the key business metrics / KPIs the system supports?
- [ ] What are the peak usage periods? (time of day, end of month, seasonal)

### 1.2 Documentation Inventory
- [ ] Access to documentation platform (Confluence, Notion, SharePoint, Wiki)
- [ ] System architecture documents
- [ ] Business requirement documents (BRDs)
- [ ] Functional specification documents (FSDs)
- [ ] User stories / acceptance criteria backlog
- [ ] API contracts / Swagger / OpenAPI specs
- [ ] Data dictionary / data model documentation
- [ ] Runbooks / SOPs (Standard Operating Procedures)
- [ ] Incident response playbooks
- [ ] Onboarding guides (existing ones, even if outdated)
- [ ] Meeting notes / decision logs from key architecture decisions
- [ ] Vendor contracts / SLA docs for third-party services

### 1.3 Team & Communication
- [ ] Organization chart — who owns what
- [ ] Key contacts: BA, QA, DevOps, DBA, PM, Architect, Security
- [ ] Escalation path for production incidents
- [ ] Escalation path for business questions
- [ ] All communication channels (Slack, Teams, email groups, war rooms)
- [ ] Meeting cadence (daily standup, sprint planning, backlog grooming, retro, demo)
- [ ] On-call rotation and coverage schedule
- [ ] Time zones of distributed team members
- [ ] Vacation / OOO coverage plan

